| **Approach:** • Introduce with the constitutional position of Vice-President as Chairman of Rajya Sabha. <br>• In the body, first explain the functional responsibilities of the Chairman, followed by describing the removal procedure with constitutional provisions. <br>• Conclude with contemporary relevance of these provisions through recent examples. |
| --- |

The office of Vice President (VP) is the **second-highest constitutional office** after the president. It is **modelled** on the lines of the **American Vice-President**. **Article 64** of the Constitution states that the VP shall also be the **ex-officio Chairperson of the Rajya Sabha**.

The **dual responsibility** encompasses a range of significant functions:
1. a) Ensures orderly conduct of House proceedings through **procedural oversight** (allocation of time for debates and discussions);
   b) provides **administrative supervision** over the functioning of Rajya Sabha Secretariat;
   c) **refers bills** to parliamentary committees based on subject matter expertise.

2. a) Maintains **institutional autonomy** of Rajya Sabha through **independent interpretation** of Rules of Procedure (Chairman's rulings become precedents for future reference);
   b) maintains decorum through **disciplinary powers** (can force a withdrawal of members; recommend them for suspension);
   c) casts a **deciding vote in case of a tie**, ensuring legislative impartiality (Article 100).

3. a) Protects **privileges** and rights of house members (Article 105);
   b) ensures **equitable participation** across political parties (proportional time allocation based on party strength);
   c) facilitates **consensus-building** on contentious issues (through all-party meetings and informal consultations).

4. a) Maintains **balance** between government business and agenda of individual legislators (can shift private members’ bills to any other day if there is no sitting on a Friday);
   b) upholds **constitutional** **provisions** regarding legislative procedures (Articles 107-111);c) **moderates debates** with impartiality ;
   d) adapts house procedures to **emerging challenges** (facilitated a hybrid model during Covid-19).

A **notice** for the motion of **removal** against the Rajya Sabha Chairman was given in December 2024. It has put the spotlight on the **removal procedure** for the Vice-President. The **procedure i**s as follows:
1. It requires a **resolution** to be introduced **only in Rajya Sabha** with minimum **14 days' notice (Article** **67(b))**.

2. Removal proceedings are not **presided by the Chairman**, following the principle of **natural justice** (nemo judex in causa sua).

3. The resolution for removal needs **approval** by a **majority of the then** membership (effective majority) of Rajya Sabha. Further, the **Lok Sabha** must also **agree** (simple majority) to such a resolution.

4. The procedure differs from Presidential impeachment as **no specific grounds** are mentioned in the Constitution (Article 67 vs Article 61).

Though the Vice-President **does not have executive powers**, the office remains **crucial for legislative stability** and political continuity, particularly in **presiding over Rajya Sabha** and assuming the **President’s role in case** **of vacancy**. ***(391 words)***

| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer by defining delimitation. <br>• In the body, first highlight its importance. <br>• Next, discuss challenges associated with the delimitation exercise in India. <br>• Conclude by underlining the need for consensus in the impending delimitation exercise. |
| --- |

**Delimitation** means the process of **fixing limits or boundaries** of **territorial constituencies** of a legislative body such as Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assembly. In **India**, the task of delimitation is assigned to a **Delimitation Commission**, a statutory body that has been **periodically constituted (1952, 1963, 1973 and** **2002).**

Significance of the delimitation exercise can be gauged from the fact that it is **prescribed in the Constitution** itself (**Article 82**). It is **important** due to following reasons:
1. a) Re-emphasizes the **principle of political equality**, i.e., “one person, one vote, one value.”;
   b) corrects **“malapportionment”** in Parliament/Assemblies, ensuring **equitable protection** of interests of states/regions.

2. a) Enables **increase in the number of elected representatives** for **effective representation** (rise in the average population per seat);
   b) corrects **distortion in size of constituencies** by accounting for **other demographic changes** (intra-state and inter-state migration, urbanization).

3. Examines need to **increase** **representation for marginalized communities** such as SCs and STs (Article 330,332). This is necessary to **adjust to population growth rate** among them and on account of **more communities getting added to the list** (Article 341,342).

4. Provisions of **106th Constitutional Amendment (women’s reservation** in Parliament/State Legislatures) can be enforced only after **post-Census delimitation**.

However, the following **challenges** are associated with the delimitation exercise:
1. a) States with **effective family planning programs** will see **decreased representation** (E.g. southern states);
   b) may **fuel regionalism**, creating a **north-south divide**. E.g. TN assembly resolution against delimitation.

2. a) **Smaller states** (Punjab, Goa) may get disadvantaged compared to **larger states** (Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Maharashtra);
   b) delimitation process in **sensitive areas** may aggravate **security risks** (north- eastern states).

3. a) May marginalize **Prime Ministerial aspirations** from **smaller states**;
   b) reinforce the **high political** **significance** of “**Hindi heartland**” in national politics;
   b) greater increase in seats from **urban areas** compared to **rural areas** (expected increase in urban population to 40% by 2036- World Bank).

4. a) Delimitation **changes political equations** by altering **demographic concentration** and l**ocal** **power dynamics** (Assam delimitation 2023); re-organization of constituencies may **not align with** **existing boundaries** creating **administrative difficulties (**J&K delimitation exercise 2022).

As per **Article 82,** the **next delimitation exercise** is supposed to be undertaken **after 2026.** In a democratic set up, it is important that the **upcoming exercise** is carried out **with consensus** to realize the vision of the founding fathers in the **Amrit Kaal.** (***375 words***)

| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer with a brief description about SEBI. <br>• In the body, examine its role in regulating the securities market and protecting investors’ interests, highlighting both successes and shortcomings. <br>• Conclude by suggesting improvements. |
| --- |

The **Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI**) is the **regulatory body** that oversees the f**unctioning of** **the capital market**, protects the **interests of investors** and ensures the **market operates in a fair and** **transparent manne**r. It derives its **powers primarily from the SEBI Act, 1992**. To fulfill its legislative mandate, the following **steps** have been taken by the SEBI:
1. a) Introduced reforms to **strengthen the regulatory framework**. E.g. insider trading regulations, implementation of the electronic trading system;
   b) brought in **ease of access** (pioneering T+0 settlement).

2. a) Ensured **ease of** **compliance** for market participants (exempting FPIs from additional disclosure requirements);
   b) developed dedicated **grievance redressal mechanism** (SCORES);
   c) has taken **action against rating agencies** (E.g. India Ratings & Research) for failure to adhere to circular requirements.

3. a) Instituted i**nvestor protection measures** (time stamping of contracts, investor protection fund, regulation of algo trading);
   b) has undertaken **innovative market mechanisms** (Social stock exchange);
   c) integrated **sustainability into business operations** (Business Responsibility & Sustainability Reporting).

4. a) Displayed proactiveness in **adapting to emerging trends** (constituted the Committee on Financial and Regulatory Technologies);
   b) taken steps to **align** with **global standards** (IOSCO principles);
   c) collaborated with international peers for **cross-border investigations (**bi-lateral MoUs with securities regulators of 29 countries).

5. a) Enabled improvements in market **efficiency and transparency** (disclosure frameworks, listing norms);
   b) has introduced **computerization** of stock exchanges **reducing transaction costs**.

Despite these credible achievements, the following **shortcomings** have been observed in SEBI’s functioning:
1. a) Challenges in timely detection and prevention of **insider trading** (ABC Bearings case, 2023);
   b) gaps in the **regulation of market intermediaries** (brokers, portfolio managers);
   c) **lack of timely action** against alleged violation of regulations (IL&FS crisis, stock price manipulation by Adani group).

2. a) Deficiencies in **investor protection framework** resulting in significant **losses for retail investors** (short-selling after Hindenburg report, F&O trading);
   b) inability to curb the **proliferation of** **"finfluencers"** spreading misinformation;
   c) issues related to **IPO overpricing.**

3. a) Limited **consultation with market participants** resulting in **restrictive regulations** (delayed operationalization of REITs);
   b) allegation of **conflict of interest** against key SEBI officials eroding **public trust** in the body;
   c) **limited penetration of capital market** (only 5% of Indians invest in markets).

**SEBI's efforts** in regulating India's capital market **are notable, but challenges persist.** **Strengthening** **regulatory framework, enforcement capability** and a more **proactive approach** in detecting and preventing market manipulation are imperative for enhancing its efficacy. *(****392 words)***

| **Approach:** • Give an introduction providing a brief background about the National Human Rights Commission (NHRC). <br>• In the body, first discuss various limitations that hinder it from effectively promoting and protecting human rights. <br>• Next, suggest measures to improve its functioning. <br>• Conclude by highlighting the significance of these reforms. |
| --- |

National Human Rights Commission (NHRC) is a **statutory** body established under the **Protection of Human** **Rights Act, 1993.** In conformity with the **Paris Principles,** NHRC is an embodiment of **India’s concern for** **promotion and protection of human rights**. However, the following **structural and practical limitations** hinder the effective performance of its role:
1. a) Considered a “**toothless tiger**” on account of the **non-binding** nature of its recommendations;
   b) lack of **enforcement capabilities** and inability to **punish violators;**
   c) limited to investigating cases within **one year of occurrence** (unless exceptional circumstances justify relaxation).

2. a) **Limited jurisdiction** over human rights violations by **armed forces** ( can only seek a report, only make recommendations**) and** private individuals or entities**;**
   b) concerns about **independence from** **the government** (membership, appointment procedure);
   c) lack of **sufficient diversity** among members of the Commission (women, tribals, minorities).

3. a) Insufficient **resource base** (manpower, financial), coupled with an **overwhelming workload**, hinder its ability to fulfill its mandate;
   b) **low public awareness** about the human rights regime in India translates to **limited public engagement** with the NHRC.

These challenges underline the need to implement the following **reforms** to improve its functioning:
1. a) Strengthening NHRC's authority by **mandating government compliance** with its recommendations;
   b) ensuring **flexibility in accepting older cases** where justified (recommended by A.M. Ahmadi Committee);
   c) imposing reasonable **time limits** to enhance the credibility of investigations.

2. a) **Expanding investigative authority** to include cases involving para-military forces, private entities or individuals;
   b) **diversifying the membership** through greater involvement of civil society members, human rights activists, and experts;
   c) **capacity building** of members/staff for handling complex cases of human rights’ violations.

3. a) Equipping the Commission with necessary **resource base** (manpower, finances);
   b) guaranteeing its **financial independence** from the Government (reducing reliance on Central government grants under Section 32).

4. a) Propagation of **human rights education** among the masses to develop a **culture of respect for** **human rights**;
   b) adherence to **international standards** (GANHRI) which will help raise institutional credibility in the public mind.

Implementing these reforms will better equip the Commission to perform its role as a **guardian** **of human rights** in India. A stronger NHRC will be able to uphold its motto “**Sarve Bhavantu Sukhinah”**  and ensure a **life of** **dignity** to every Indian. ***(396 words)***

***GANHRI:** Global Alliance of National Human Rights Institutions.

| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer by giving a brief background about farmer associations in India. <br>• In the body, discuss their role in influencing public policies and governance in India. <br>• Next, bring out their shortcomings. <br>• Conclude by emphasizing their crucial role in governance and policy formulation. |
| --- |

**Farmers' associations** in India have a **long history of advocacy and activism**, dating back to the **pre-** **independence era (**All India Kisan Sabha, UP Kisan Sabha). Also, farmer leaders have gone on to hold **key** **constitutional positions** (former PM Ch. Charan Singh), shaping government policy in all spheres of activity. **Farmer associations** have often played a **crucial role** in **influencing public policies and governance** in the following ways:
1. a) Successes in price realization** due to strong political influence (sugarcane farmers in western UP);**
   b) shaping **land reform policies** post-independence;
   c) advocating **policies and laws to their** **benefit.** E.g. MSP, credit, irrigation, input subsidy;
   d) **Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and** **Resettlement Act (LARR Act), 2013** was an outcome of a major farmer agitation in Bhatta-Parsaul.

2. a) Leveraged their **political significance** to inspire dedicated schemes for farmers (PM Kisan scheme before 2019 elections);
   b) close **ties with political parties** (E.g. BKS, AIKS);
   c) engagement in **negotiations, quick mobilization capability**. E.g. Samyukta Kisan Morcha (Non-Political), Kisan Mazdoor Morcha led Delhi Chalo (2.0).

3. Influencing **foreign trade policy, FTA negotiations**. E.g. opposition of dairy farmers (RCEP, BTIA), Peace clause at WTO.

4. a) Critics** argue that farmer associations have been responsible for some of **economically and** **ecologically irrational policies** E.g. loan waivers;**
   b) they have often **opposed implementation of** **policies for sustainable agriculture** (stubble burning prohibitions, ground water regulations).

However, they have also faced **setbacks and incomplete successes**:
1. a) Failure to advocate **long term structural reforms** (marketing, land leasing, contract farming, sustainable agriculture);
   b) **more influence of large farmers**, marginal farmers unable to get proportional benefit.

2. a) **Fragmentation within farming community** (based on caste, religion, region, gender, land size);
   b) **limited resources for sustained mobilization** lowers their bargaining power. E.g. multiple splits in BKU (Ugrahan, Ekta Sidhpur).

3. a) Use of farmer associations to fulfill **political ambitions of certain groups/individuals** reduces their **credibility**;
   b) violent protests and continuous road blockages reduce public support (Farmer agitation 2020-21).

4. a) **Competition from other pressure groups** (business groups who demand greater economic liberalization and more FTAs);
   b) limitations imposed by **WTO regulations and other countries'** **policies**;
   c) **consumer bias** in policies reflected in **prioritization of inflation control** (e.g. Export restrictions, stock limits).

Overall, farmers' associations have **played a significant role** in shaping policies and governance, also, reflected in the recent conferment of **Bharat Ratna to M S Swaminathan and Chaudhary Charan Singh**. But their **influence is not absolute**, there also have been failures in ensuring that farmers' interests are adequately addressed by policymakers. (***427 words***)

| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer by giving a brief description about NCW. <br>• In the body, first highlight its key achievements in addressing gender issues in the country. <br>• Next, bring out the issues where the Commission has faced challenges. <br>• Conclude by suggesting measures to make the NCW more effective. |
| --- |

**National Commission for Women (NCW**) is a **statutory body** set up in **1992** under the **National Commission** **for Women Act, 1990**.Mandated to act as a **watchdog of gender justice** in the country, the Commission has demonstrated a **commitment to ensure the rights of women**. It has made **significant progress** in addressing gender issues in following ways:
1. a) Key role in expanding the **protective legal framework** for women (POSH Act 2013, Domestic Violence Act, 2005);
   b) advocating for **justice for victims** in several sensitive cases (Nirbhaya case, Kathua case).

2. a) Providing **support mechanisms** for women in **distress** (24*7 NCW helpline);*
   b) **connecting** women in distress with **police and legal authorities**;
   c) provision of **mental and physical health support** for victims of gender violence (One Stop Centres).

3. a) Taking **suo-moto cognizance** of reported incidents of violence/harassment against women (Anna University case);
   b) enabling **speedy and effective disposal** of women’s complaints in **collaboration** with police and legal aid authorities (Mahila Jan Sunwai).

4. a) Conducting **gender sensitization** and **capacity building programmes** for government officials in collaboration with training institutions;
   b) investment in **research activities** on issues related to women (research studies on “Cyber Security and threats in Cyber Space faced by Women” during 2020- 21).

5. a) Undertaking periodic reviews and **evaluations of legal-institutional framework** (e.g., study on conditions of women inmates in prisons);
   b) implementing initiatives to raise **awareness among** **women** about their rights (Mangalam programme in Puducherry).

Despite the NCW's significant contributions to women's empowerment, the Commission faces **several key** **challenges**:
1. a) **Non-binding** nature of the Commission’s recommendations (NCW Drafted Bill for the Victims of Acid Attacks in 2008 failed to become a law);
   b) lack of **independent budget** and dedicated human resources undermining its **autonomy** (excessive reliance on temporary appointments).

2. a) Perception of **political biases** in its functioning  (Mahua Moitra case);
   b) **sluggish response** to incidents of heinous crimes against women (Manipur);
   c) alleged **lack of sensitivity** among members of the Commission (Mangaluru pub incident 2009).

3. a) Regional disparities** in reach and impact of the Commission’s work (woes of tribal women in Maharashtra's sugarcane belt);**
   b) **limited success** regarding women's **economic empowerment** as reflected in low Female Labor Force Participation Rate.

Strengthening the **autonomy** of the NCW, granting it **constitutional status,** and **integrating technological** **solution**s can substantially enhance its effectiveness. A **stronger NCW** will help in **women’s empowerment** and achievement of the vision of **‘GYAN pe Dhyan’**. (***395 words***)

| **Approach:** • Introduce the answer with a brief description of the Interactive Service Model (ISM) of e- governance. <br>• In the body, first explain the role ISM plays in the service delivery process and in ensuring transparency and accountability. <br>• Then, identify the challenges inhibiting its successful adoption. <br>• Conclude by suggesting the use of emerging technologies to advance its adoption. |
| --- |

Interactive Service Model (ISM) of e-governance is a **framework for public-service delivery through digital** **platforms (ICT)** that enable **two-way communication** between citizens and public authorities. Unlike traditional models that focus solely on providing services or information, the interactive model emphasizes **real-** **time engagement.**

ISM plays a **multifaceted role in service delivery process**, as discussed:
1. a) Goes **beyond simply digitizing** existing services and **creates new channels** for engagement and collaboration facilitating **participatory governance** (e.g, MyGov.in);
   b) facilitates **diverse interactions** aimed at enhancing access to multiple **government services** i.e. **G2C** (e.g. UMANG);
   c) creates a **responsive government** i.e. **C2G** (e.g. e-Samvad).

2. a) Increases **ease of doing business** i.e. **G2B** (e.g. MCA 21);
   b) brings down the **inter-departmental** **communication** gap through **G2G interactions** (e.g. e-Office);
   c) fosters **innovation** through interaction among governments, citizens and private sector in **co-creating solutions** (Digital-India Hackathon).

3. a) **Enhances transparency** by providing **real-time updates** on government policies, processes, and decisions (PRAGATI platform);
   b) ensures availability of **government data** to the public, allowing for **scrutiny and analysis** (OGD-Platform India);
   c) facilitates **open decision-making processes** based on **objective criteria** e.g. online auction of government tenders (MSTC e-Commerce).

4. a) **Improves accountability** by allowing citizens to **scrutinize government’s decisions** (RTI online);
   b) empowers citizens through interactive platforms for **grievance redressal** (CPGRAMS);
   c) creates **closed-loop** systems that encourage feedback on services and promote resolution (e.g. RailMadad).

5. a) **Enhances service-delivery** through streamlined processes and improved response times (DigiLocker);
   b) **bridges the digital divide** with **multi-channel access** (web, mobile, kiosks) to cater to different user preferences **(Common-Service-Centers)**.

However, the successful implementation of the model faces the following **challenges**:
1. a) Disparity in access** **to internet** resulting in a **rural-urban divide** (only 24% of rural-households in India have internet access compared to 66% urban households-NSSO);**
   b) **digital gender divide** (only 33% women in India have ever used the internet, compared to more than half (57%) the men: NFHS-5);
   c) **public hesitancy** over **privacy concerns,** fueled by frequent public data leaks (e.g. Angel one data breach, Feb 2025).

2. a) High **digital-illiteracy** (only 24.7% household- NSS) and lack of **digital stewardship** among marginalised populations (e.g., disabled);
   b) **multiplicity of feedback** creates information overload and subdues the government’s processing capability;
   c) lackadaisical approach and **inertia from the** **bureaucracy,** hindering effective adoption.

The **Interactive Service Model** of e-governance holds immense potential to enhance **transparency,** **accountability,** and **service delivery**. However, overcoming challenges such as the digital divide and privacy concerns through emerging technologies (AI, ML, blockchain) is crucial to realizing the goal of **‘Minimum** **Government, Maximum Governance’.** ***(442 words)***

**ICT:** Information and Communication Technology**; G2C:** Government to Citizens**; UMANG:** Unified Mobile Application for New-age Governance**; C2G:** Citizens to Government**; DISHA:** Digital Interaction To Seek Help Anytime**; G2B:** Government to Business**; G2G:** Government to Government**: PRAGATI:** Pro-Active Governance and Timely Implementation**; OGD:** Open Government Data**;CPGRAMS:** Centralized Public Grievance Redress and Monitoring System**; NSSO:** National Sample Survey Office**: NFHS:** National Family Health Survey**; NSS:** National Sample Survey

