| **Approach:** • Introduce by giving a brief background about the strategic partnership between India and Russia. <br>• In the body, first examine how the changing geo-political landscape poses challenges to bilateral ties. <br>• Next, highlight how the relationship remains resilient. <br>• Conclude by suggesting measures to further deepen the relations. |
| --- |

India and Russia entered into a **Strategic Partnership Agreement** for the first time in **2000**. Elevated to the level of a **Special and Privileged Strategic Partnership** in **2010,** India-Russia ties have acquired a qualitatively new character with **enhanced levels of cooperation** in almost all areas (political, security, defence, economy, people- to-people ties) Although the ties are based on strategic convergences, common geopolitical interests, and mutually beneficial cooperation, **changing geopolitical landscape** poses challenges to the relationship in the following ways:
1. a) The **disintegration of the USSR** and the **end of the Cold War** has marked a shift in India’s foreign policy;
   b) Russia’s **declining** global position vis-a-vis the US;
   c) **convergence of strategic interests** of India and USA, e.g. Quad and Russia’ s criticism of Quad;
   d) strengthening of **Russia-Pakistan-China ties.**

2. a) The **rise of China** has fostered closer India-US ties;
   b) growing **Russia-China bonhomie** amidst US led efforts to **isolate Russia** in wake of the Ukraine crisis;
   c) Russia has often adopted a **neutral stance** in matters of **India- China conflict.** E.g. Galwan crisis.

3. a) Despite following an independent foreign policy, **the Ukraine crisis** has made it difficult for India to **continue business as usual** with Russia. E.g. the annual leaders’ summit could be held only after two years;
   b) Russian actions have been suggestive of **violation of international law (**e.g. Bucha massacre).

4. The **war has diverted Russian attention and resources away from Asia,** while also causing **delays in** **arms deliveries and defense spare parts** to India.

Despite these challenges, India-Russia ties **remain resilient** on account of below mentioned factors:
1. a) Historical experiences,** e.g. USSR support during Bangladesh liberation war;**
   b) better India-Russia ties are fueled by **‘institutional memory’** of a close cooperation;
   c) **established mechanisms** support continued resilience in ties. E.g. IRIGC, 2+2 Dialogue; Multilateral engagements through BRICS, G20, SCO.

2. a) Strategic Defence cooperation** which has evolved from a buyer-seller relationship (S-400 defence system) to one including **joint development** (BrahMos) and technology transfer;**
   b) shared interest in the establishment of a **multipolar world**;
   c) cooperation in the field of S&T, **nuclear energy and space** **cooperation.** E.g. Gaganyaan mission;
   d) collaboration on building peace in Afghanistan (Moscow Format).

3. a) Economic cooperation** reflected in India being a key investor in promoting the Russia-driven **Eastern** **Economic Forum;
   b) connectivity initiatives,** e.g. INSTC, Chennai-Vladivostok Maritime Corridor;**
